data power_decomposition_note: String = "Power decomposition v2 (service-boundary directive; supersedes the v3 PowerInputRealization whose SharedDcBus arm collapsed four independent decisions): AC/DC conversion x distribution voltage x board adaptation x branch connection, with supply multiplicity a separate policy. The formulation is ONE LOGICAL POWER SERVICE realized by a modular rectifier shelf and exact protected branches - never 'one giant PSU'; the shelf sits in the freestanding frame's service base, never on the building wall. Shelf-and-protected-manual-cables FIRST: a busbar, sliding contact, blind-mate tolerance stack, contact wipe, arc-safe insertion, connector float, or hot-plug behavior is invented only when ordinary protected cables produce a MEASURED maintenance, clutter, voltage-drop, or reliability problem (the rev-0 blind-mate refusal survives: ManualKeyedCable on all current candidates). Redundancy is a separate policy decision, not implied by sharing. A Jade arriving with two CRPS modules while the plan runs one is a Jade-realization property, never architecture; the air reference carries no dormant redundant-power dependency. Shared12V vs Shared48V is structural neither-wins: 12V = high distribution current, no per-node conversion, short-domain preference (even four 350-450W nodes ~117-150A at 12V before margin - a serious distribution system); 48V = 16x lower resistive loss, but one BOARD-FAMILY-QUALIFIED DC/DC module per 12V board with its own continuous/transient/thermal/regulation/inrush/fault/availability/connector obligations - converter cost is a market-search hypothesis, never a design premise."

data power_contract_standing_note: String = "Electrical-contract standing ladder (service-boundary directive): Direct12VSupported - OBSERVED from the official ASRock Rack product authority (asrockrack.com product page, review-verified 2026-08-10; extdeps pinning owed). Direct12VInterfaceComplete - UNRESOLVED pending official manual/design facts as extdeps rows: input count and pinout, allowable range, peak and transient current, ripple, startup/inrush, standby/BMC behavior, grounding, connector temperature rise, required sequencing, fault response. Shared12VReady - UNRESOLVED pending design + prototype receipts. 'The bus just delivers clean 12V' is RETRACTED as too strong; the product page proves the mode exists, not the contract."

data power_safety_note: String = "SAFETY STANDING (retraction, service-boundary directive): the session claim 'below 60V DC is SELV - safe-to-touch' is RETRACTED. Low voltage is not low energy: a 12/48V system carries fault energy sufficient to melt tools, ignite insulation, damage connectors, burn, and collapse the power domain (OCP 48V contacts are rated to hundreds of amps). The correct claim: moving mains AC into one listed rectifier enclosure MAY reduce the number of mains-voltage boundaries; the downstream DC system still requires qualified protection, covering, bonding, branch isolation, and fault-energy analysis. SELV is an earned classification under the applicable authority, never an assumed admission receipt. Battery correction: a battery service is NEVER a passive wire on the bus - it carries its own dependency population (chemistry, battery management, charge control, disconnect, overcurrent protection, thermal monitoring, state-of-charge observation, end-of-life replacement, fault containment) and enters only as its own service with a full ledger."

data power_prototype_ladder_note: String = "Prototype ladder (replaces the session's preselected '3-6 nodes on 12V'): P0 one ALTRAD8UD on an ordinary listed ATX PSU; P1 one ALTRAD8UD on the official direct-12V path; P2 TWO nodes on one shared 12V rectifier domain; P3 expand only after branch-fault, voltage-drop, inrush, thermal, noise, and efficiency receipts; P4 compare 48V distribution only when 12V distribution burden is OBSERVED, not predicted. Comparison axes vs the per-node ATX baseline: efficiency, acoustic effect, branch fault isolation, voltage regulation, inrush behavior, connector/conductor temperature, maintenance actions, dependency count, reproduction time, domain blast radius, build and operating cost. The successful result is not 'shared power won' - it is one exact receipt showing which service boundary is simplest for this node count, wire length, workload, and site. Mining-style PSU breakout boards are lab candidates at most until qualified; shared-rectifier efficiency advantage is measured, not assumed. 'Shared bus = one software drain domain' is an excellent TESTABLE candidate, not a universal law (acceptance control 4 tests it)."

data power_service_candidates: List<PowerServiceCandidate> = [
  PowerServiceCandidate {
    id: "cand-power-shared12",
    conversion: SharedRectifierShelf,
    distribution: Distribution12V,
    board_adaptation: DirectBoard12V,
    connection: ManualKeyedCable,
    note: "P1/P2 candidate: listed/modular rectifier shelf in the frame service base -> covered fused distribution block -> one manually disconnectable protected branch per carrier -> board direct-12V input. Emits NO converter dependency (acceptance control 9).",
    status: HypothesisGrade { note: "gated on Direct12VInterfaceComplete + P1/P2 receipts" },
  },
  PowerServiceCandidate {
    id: "cand-power-shared48",
    conversion: SharedRectifierShelf,
    distribution: Distribution48V,
    board_adaptation: Local48To12Converter,
    connection: ManualKeyedCable,
    note: "P4 candidate: larger practical domain, 16x lower distribution loss; emits exactly one board-family-qualified 48->12 converter dependency per attached 12V board (acceptance control 10) - conversion loss and heat land at each carrier. Compared only when 12V burden is observed.",
    status: HypothesisGrade { note: "deliberately unprototyped until P4; converter qualification is the gating dependency" },
  },
]

