data network_burden_note: String = "NETWORK BURDEN IS FIRST-CLASS (maintenance-locality directive; the operator's caveat that offload silently moves requirements onto the network). Every StorageBinding MUST emit a NetworkServiceDemand. Model TWO distinct traffic populations, because monthly bytes lie: (1) cold-start/materialization - ColdBytesPerPeriod = consumer_starts x bytes_per_materialization x remote_miss_fraction, with the herd envelope RequiredBootBandwidth = simultaneous_consumers x image_bytes / admitted_boot_window; (2) steady state = read_bytes_rate + write_bytes_rate + protocol_overhead + metadata_operation_rate. Worked example (illustrative): 20 nodes x 2GB image x 4 boots/month = 160 GB/month ~ 0.5 Mbps average - invisible; the SAME bytes recovered inside a 2-minute window after a site-wide power event = 20 x 2GB / 120s ~ 333 MB/s ~ 2.7 Gbps. Same population, completely different network requirement - so every demand carries average, p95, maximum synchronized burst, AND required completion window. CONSERVATION LAW: sum(admitted steady traffic) + admitted concurrent burst envelope + control-plane reserve + customer-job network reserve <= OBSERVED USABLE fabric capacity (never NIC nameplate, and measured under the selected protocol and concurrency). Model at least three bottlenecks separately - HostEdgeCapacity, SharedFabricCapacity, StorageProviderCapacity: a 100 GbE uplink does not help if nodes connect at 10 GbE, the storage server has 25 GbE, the pool sustains 8 GB/s, or protocol CPU saturates first. Traffic classes follow: a site-wide reboot must never crowd out control-plane/BMC traffic or the egress required to drain jobs."

type RepairBatchability
  = MustRepairImmediately
  | DeferrableToScheduledVisit
  | BatchableAcrossDevices

type OperatorInterventionProfile {
  id: String
  realization_id: String
  expected_hands_on_minutes_per_year_note: String
  expected_emergency_visits_per_year_note: String
  expected_scheduled_visits_per_year_note: String
  replacement_procedure_count_note: String
  unique_procedure_count_note: String
  median_recovery_minutes_note: String
  p95_recovery_minutes_note: String
  batchability: RepairBatchability
  remote_recovery_fraction_note: String
  consumers_affected_during_repair_note: String
  sites_visited_and_travel_note: String
  special_tools_note: String
}

data maintenance_locality_note: String = "MAINTENANCE LOCALITY IS FIRST-CLASS (maintenance-locality directive; the operator's strongest offload argument). Every local/shared realization MUST emit an OperatorInterventionProfile. ExpectedLocalMaintenanceHours = device_population x annual_failure_probability x (diagnosis + drain + physical access + replacement + reinstall/restore + validation minutes) + per-node upgrade work + configuration-drift incidents. ExpectedSharedMaintenanceHours = pool_device_population x AFR x pool_replacement_minutes + storage-service upgrade hours + monitoring/scrub hours + shared incident hours. But minutes UNDER-DESCRIBE the difference: emergency fraction, deferability, batchability, sites that must be visited, travel time, special tools, unique procedures, and consumers affected during repair all count. The decisive contrast: 'replace three failed/aged drives during one planned visit next week' versus 'find the machine, drain it now, remove the carrier, replace the M.2, reinstall it, verify its identity' - that difference in urgency and batchability may be worth more than the raw replacement minutes. Honesty guard: centralization does NOT eliminate drive maintenance; it relocates and amortizes it, and adds a storage service to operate plus a shared blast radius."

