data boot_decomposition_note: String = "BOOT DECOMPOSED (maintenance-locality directive; richer than the session's flat NoLocalReplica). Authority x materialization x availability requirement. The load-bearing field is may_running_nodes_depend_on_service: setting it FALSE ('running_node_continues_when_boot_service_unavailable = true') naturally admits RAM-root and full local caching while REFUSING an always-live remote root for the ordinary compute fleet. Candidate materializations: NetworkToRamNoNodeCache, NetworkToRamWithSiteCache, NetworkToLocalEphemeralRoot, SanBootPersistentRoot, NfsMountedRoot. Why RAM-root over NFS root: a root fully materialized into RAM limits the network dependency to the BOOT WINDOW, whereas NFS root keeps the network on the node's continuous execution path - losing the server hangs a RUNNING node. External analogues (readings, pin-verification owed): Warewulf provisions a shared OS image over the network into node memory explicitly to end per-node installation and version drift; Cisco UCS SAN boot lets a service profile move to different hardware and appear as the same server (logical host identity != motherboard != boot drive != carrier - the 'node becomes a slot' property); Azure ephemeral OS disks pair an upstream image authority WITH a local ephemeral runtime image, including a full-local-cache mode that removes steady-state remote reads - the two ideas are not opposites; Kubernetes accounts local ephemeral storage as scheduled capacity applications must tolerate losing with the node; NVMe-oF makes remote scratch legitimate but moves the bar from cheap central disks to a low-latency high-bandwidth fabric that may cost more than the local NVMe population it replaces at current scale."

type LocalScratchServiceability {
  id: String
  realization_note: String
  no_authoritative_state: Bool
  replacement_requires_no_reinstall: Bool
  replacement_requires_no_board_removal: Bool
  tool_count_note: String
  maximum_hands_on_minutes_note: String
  hot_swap_required: Bool
  spare_on_site: Bool
}

data scratch_serviceability_note: String = "THE REPAIR PATH IS PART OF STORAGE DESIGN, NOT AN AFTERTHOUGHT (maintenance-locality directive). Keeping local scratch does not require accepting a buried M.2 repair path. Realizations to compare: onboard M.2, PCIe NVMe card, front-access U.2/U.3, EDSFF carrier, and a SMALL FRAME-LOCAL SCRATCH SHELF where several boards consume independently allocated namespaces from one physically accessible unit - a shared dependency, but site-local low latency with concentrated repair; compared against direct-attached NVMe, never assumed. The cheapest device is not the cheapest lifecycle realization if replacing it requires disassembling the compute carrier. Local NVMe standing, in all cases: disposable, schedulable capacity, NO completed-job authority, replace-and-reboot recovery (blank-device swap, no install, no restore), physically serviceable where practical - reclassified from a ComputeAssembly property to a selected scratch service binding."

type BootScratchCandidate {
  id: String
  label: String
  boot_placement: BootRuntimePlacement
  scratch_placement: StoragePlacement
  note: String
  status: RealizationStatus
}

data boot_scratch_candidates: List<BootScratchCandidate> = [
  BootScratchCandidate {
    id: "cand-A-local-boot-local-scratch",
    label: "A - fully local traditional node (CONTROL)",
    boot_placement: LocalEphemeralRoot,
    scratch_placement: LocalDevice,
    note: "Local boot drive, local OS, local scratch. Minimal network requirement, strongest local independence; costs per-node installations, version drift, boot-device repair, and per-node identity. Retained as the conventional control against which the others must prove they buy something real.",
    status: HypothesisGrade { note: "control arm" },
  },
  BootScratchCandidate {
    id: "cand-B-netboot-ram-local-scratch",
    label: "B - network boot to RAM + local NVMe scratch (EXPECTED INITIAL WINNER)",
    boot_placement: RamRoot,
    scratch_placement: LocalDevice,
    note: "Content-addressed OS authority upstream -> network materialization at boot -> RAM-root runtime; local NVMe as disposable scratch ONLY. Buys: no per-node OS installation, no OS drive, no version drift, running nodes independent of the boot service, fast private scratch, and NVMe failure requiring no restore or reinstall. Costs: boot-service dependency during startup, boot-herd network burst, RAM consumed by the root image, and a local scratch device still eventually needing physical replacement. Recorded as the HYPOTHESIS most likely to preserve both goals (centralized image authority with near-zero node-specific administration, plus private low-latency scratch) - NOT a stored verdict.",
    status: HypothesisGrade { note: "preferred initial candidate pending measurement" },
  },
  BootScratchCandidate {
    id: "cand-C-netboot-ram-tmpfs-scratch",
    label: "C - network boot to RAM + tmpfs scratch",
    boot_placement: RamRoot,
    scratch_placement: MemoryBacked,
    note: "Genuinely diskless node: no local drive repairs, fastest low-latency scratch. Costs sellable RAM (at 1:3 packing on 512GB, RAM is the vCPU-binding constraint per compute-infrastructure section 7 - every GB to tmpfs is capacity not sold), scratch vanishes on reset, and memory pressure becomes a workload-admission constraint. Admitted as a SCHEDULER-SELECTED realization for small jobs or abundant-RAM periods; must NOT become the universal node design while RAM is the packing constraint. Consistent with the schedulable-memory thesis: scratch placement is a scheduling variable, not a fixed hardware fact.",
    status: HypothesisGrade { note: "scheduler-selectable, not a universal design" },
  },
  BootScratchCandidate {
    id: "cand-D-netboot-remote-scratch",
    label: "D - network boot + remote scratch",
    boot_placement: RamRoot,
    scratch_placement: SiteSharedService,
    note: "Scratch over NVMe-oF / network filesystem / block service. Buys: no local drives, all repair and capacity work concentrated in one storage service. Costs: fabric latency, shared throughput, storage-service operations, larger blast radius, continuous network dependence. May win later with 25/100 GbE, a measured workload, and enough node population that maintenance concentration justifies the fabric - not obviously the cheapest initial realization.",
    status: HypothesisGrade { note: "instrumented future candidate; gated on the diskless promotion gate" },
  },
]

data diskless_promotion_gate: String = "PROMOTION GATE (maintenance-locality directive): remove the local NVMe only when MEASURED operator time saved by centralized repair exceeds the network/fabric cost, the lost sellable RAM or scratch performance, and the added shared-failure burden. Eight controls owed as witnesses: (1) boot authority has ONE content hash regardless of node count; (2) running RAM-root nodes survive loss of the boot service; (3) boot-service loss blocks only new/recovering nodes; (4) a herd restart cannot exceed the admitted network envelope; (5) local scratch loss loses no completed artifact; (6) removing local scratch adds the EXACT remote-scratch network demand; (7) adding a local cache lowers upstream bytes but does NOT create a second semantic authority; (8) replacing any physical node requires no node-specific reinstall or data restoration."

type StorageBinding {
  id: String
  demand_id: String
  provider_id: String
  placement: StoragePlacement
  local_replication: ReplicaPolicy
  network_path_note: String
  failure_domain_note: String
  recovery: RecoveryBehavior
  evidence: EvidenceAnchor
}

data storage_service_note: String = "Storage unbundled and RESTRUCTURED into orthogonal facts (service-boundary directive, then contamination-access-storage directive section 7). ComputeAssembly.local_storage was the inherited-bundle defect this thread removes, reproduced in our own v3; the v4 replacement then reproduced it at type level - a flat StorageRealization enum (LocalEphemeralNvme | RemoteDurableObject | SiteSharedFilesystem | LocalContentAddressedCache | ...) fuses independent decisions and grows combinatorially. Now: mutually exclusive meanings are coproducts (a binding has exactly ONE DataStanding), orthogonal decisions are products - DataStanding x AccessSemantics x StoragePlacement x Mutability x Durability x Isolation x observed access pattern x performance requirement, composed by StorageDemand and bound by StorageBinding. role_label is a human-readable workload role (boot image, job scratch, artifact, ...), never the authority - the orthogonal facts decide placement. Hot and cold are OBSERVED RATES, not permanent semantic types: today's hot cache is tomorrow's unused object. Governing shape, preserved: MOVE STORAGE AUTHORITY UPSTREAM; RETAIN LOCAL STORAGE ONLY WHERE LOCALITY ITSELF DISCHARGES A PERFORMANCE, ISOLATION, OR RECOVERY REQUIREMENT - more precise than either 'all storage remote' or 'every server owns its disks'. The local NVMe is a scratch appliance attached to a compute assembly; destroying it must never destroy the semantic result of a completed job."

data storage_placement_arithmetic_note: String = "SCREENING MODEL (contamination-access-storage directive section 3; CORRECTS the session's 'total operations x latency difference', which is an UPPER BOUND holding only when every operation serializes on the critical path): dT ~ N_serial x (L_remote - L_local) + B_read x (1/R_remote_read - 1/R_local_read) + B_write x (1/R_remote_write - 1/R_local_write) + d_queueing + d_protocol_cpu + d_synchronization, where N_serial counts causally dependent, non-overlapped, non-cached round trips; B is bytes on the critical path; R is REALIZED throughput, never link nameplate; queueing includes cross-node interference; protocol CPU includes filesystem, encryption, checksumming, and client/server work. At a 250us increment: 5,000 serial misses ~1.25s, 100,000 ~25s, 1,000,000 ~250s - so the session's ~300s figure was a plausible worst case, not a consequence of counting a million filesystem operations. Page cache, metadata caching, read-ahead, batching, async writes, and parallel compilation reduce seriality; synchronous metadata chains and lock-heavy flows restore it. BANDWIDTH: aggregate local ~ sum of each active node's usable device bandwidth; aggregate shared <= min(fabric, server interfaces, media, storage software, per-client limits) - a 10Gb/s link is 1.25 GB/s theoretical before overhead, so replacing many local devices with one network path trades N independent queues for one contended queue: right for low-duty durable traffic, potentially very wrong for parallel scratch. COST is multi-axis, never one offload score: local lifecycle = nodes x (device amortization + expected replacement + power + spares + operator replacement work); shared/upstream lifecycle = capacity-time + operations + transferred bytes + network capacity + provider/cluster operations + required replication + shared-failure-domain cost. Benefit (durability/recovery, dedup, deleted per-node dependency burden, easier node replacement) is weighed against burden (critical-path latency, contention, shared blast radius, provider/control-plane dependency, request/transfer cost, tenant isolation complexity). Capacity is cheap; DURABLE, HIGH-PERFORMANCE, SHARED storage is not - the session conflated device price with service cost."

data storage_tendency_note: String = "DEMOTED CLAIM (contamination-access-storage directive section 4): the session's 'temperature anti-correlates with authority' is NOT a law - counterexamples include an actively written database, a write-ahead log, a live message queue, a frequently updated source-of-truth index, and a low-traffic but disposable cached image. The honest form, scoped to the selected CI SKU: most extremely hot filesystem activity is job-local and reconstructible, while most outputs that must survive are transferred only a few times. That tendency is enough to justify the architecture; it must never be quoted as a universal principle. Also demoted: 'scratch and caches are local, always' (site-shared caches with local hot replicas are a real candidate, replication decided by observed reuse) and 'artifact upload is literally one operation' (rhetorical overclaim)."

data storage_experiment_note: String = "PLACEMENT EXPERIMENT (supersedes generic filesystem-operation reasoning; contamination-access-storage directive section 6). Same clean build, same hardware, three arms: local NVMe / tmpfs lower-bound control / candidate network filesystem or block service. Observe: end-to-end wall time, CPU utilization, bytes read+written, metadata operation population, serial filesystem wait, p50/p95/p99 operation latency, network bytes, network saturation, storage-server queue depth, cross-node interference, result correctness. Differential controls: (1) one build alone; (2) multiple builds on separate local devices; (3) multiple builds sharing the remote service; (4) warm vs cold cache; (5) metadata-heavy incremental build; (6) large sequential artifact upload; (7) storage-service interruption and node loss. Expected shape of the answer, NOT assumed: remote artifact path passes; remote scratch path passes or refuses; shared cache passes only up to an observed concurrency."

data semantic_authority_note: String = "SHARPENED FRAMING (maintenance-locality directive; corrects the session's 'dual authority' wording): prefer ONE SEMANTIC AUTHORITY WITH MULTIPLE LOCALITY REALIZATIONS - two writable authorities imply reconciliation. Upstream owns canonical image, artifact, durable state, and cache identity; the local realization (RAM-loaded image, disposable scratch, cached object, temporary write layer) exists ONLY because locality discharges a requirement - latency, bandwidth, continued operation during a network interruption, or fault isolation - and otherwise disappears. Generalized law: CENTRALIZE SEMANTIC AUTHORITY AND REPETITIVE MAINTENANCE; LOCALIZE ONLY THE PHYSICAL REALIZATION REQUIRED FOR PERFORMANCE, ISOLATION, OR DISCONNECTED CONTINUITY. Concern -> upstream authority / local realization: OS -> signed versioned image / RAM-root or ephemeral local root; scratch -> none beyond the job contract / NVMe or tmpfs; cache -> content identity + origin / site or node replica; artifacts -> durable object authority / bounded upload buffer; configuration -> fleet profile / boot-time rendered config; secrets -> secret authority / short-lived token; access control -> identity + policy / locally cached door decision; monitoring -> durable event history / local bounded buffer; DNS+content -> origin authority / cache; power -> rectifier/domain authority / protected local branch + conversion."

data offload_displacement_law: String = "OPERATOR CAVEAT, RULED AS LAW (maintenance-locality directive): a design is NOT simpler because it has fewer disks - it is simpler only when the maintenance it removes is worth the network and shared-service obligations it creates. Offload is a DEPENDENCY DISPLACEMENT, never a deletion: per-node device + per-node replacement + per-node imaging/state drift + low-latency private bandwidth becomes network-path dependency + shared-provider dependency + centralized drive replacement + shared protection/redundancy + easier capacity growth + possible shared contention. Both sides stay in the ledger, and the model must ask: what human maintenance and repeated authority are removed, and what network load and shared-service operations are introduced? Network boot very likely passes this test for the operating system; fully remote scratch might or might not - the model must make that difference impossible to hand-wave. OffloadBenefit rows: operator hours removed, emergency interventions removed, node identity removed, rebuild steps removed, capacity scaling simplified, replacement batchability gained. OffloadBurden rows: network average, network peak, boot-storm requirement, shared-service operations, shared-failure blast radius, storage-fabric capex, network power, latency and contention. Do NOT collapse these into dollars too early - 'requires an emergency trip at 2 a.m.' is a qualitative operating constraint, not a line item."

data vocabulary_debt_note: String = "VOCABULARY DEBT RULE (operator coproduct concern, ruled in the contamination-access-storage directive section 7). The rule is NOT 'an enum arm may never precede a consumer' - shape search requires vocabulary before the first instance. The rule IS: an uninstantiated arm has NO OPERATIONAL AUTHORITY, generates no artifact and no dependency, and carries a dissolution trigger if continued absence indicates speculative vocabulary. Composition discipline alongside it: mutually exclusive meanings are coproducts; orthogonal decisions are products, never a fused enum (the retired StorageRealization is the worked example). Together these keep shape search possible without letting the type universe become an unreviewed roadmap. Standing debt: most PhysicalFunction arms remain uninstantiated - see seed_demands for the ones that now have exact consumers."

